  "subject": "🦅 Axios Finish Line: Service after service",
  "date": "Tue, 10 Nov 2026 21:29:26 -0500",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🏞️ Plus: Park du jour | Tuesday, November 10, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Nov 10, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n Welcome back! Smart Brevity™ count: 257 words … 1 min. Copy edited by Amy Stern. \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: Veterans who keep serving \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Veterans volunteer, help neighbors and give to charity more than the average American.\n\n Why it matters: For many veterans, service is a way of life — and it continues long after they've hung up their uniforms. 💡 Data du jour: Veterans who volunteer spend an average of 93 hours a year serving, compared with 69 hours for other volunteers, according to the 2025 Veterans Civic Health Index . \n\n Americans who've served are also likelier to donate to charity (56% vs. 50% of nonveterans). Veterans are also slightly more likely to talk to their neighbors and help them with transportation, errands or child care. Zoom in: On and around Veterans Day, many veterans turn that spirit of giving back toward their own communities.\n\n 🍜 In San Diego, veterans, active-duty service members and other volunteers organized a food drive on Sunday for military families struggling to make ends meet. 🦃 In southeastern Michigan, VETLIFE — a veteran-founded nonprofit — is working with Kroger to provide free Thanksgiving dinners to 400 veteran and active-duty military families. 🔨 In Sacramento, veterans are teaming up tomorrow to repair fellow veterans' homes through Habitat for Humanity's Veterans Service Day. The bottom line: Tomorrow is a chance to honor and say thanks to some of the most generous members of our community.\n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM AXIOS\r\n\n \r\n See what's next for media in 2027 \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Media Trends Executive Annual members receive the 2026 Annual Report, our special media document featuring global forecasts, sector analysis and exclusive media data insights.\r\n\n\r\n \r\n🔒 Get the report by becoming a Media Trends Executive member.\r\n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. ⛰️ Parting shot! \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Photo: Joe Capraro Finish Line readers Madi and Joe Capraro honeymooned in national parks in September.\n\n Here's one of their shots: sunrise at Oxbow Bend in Grand Teton National Park. \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
